# Plan: customer satisfaction rating

Spec: `docs/specs/csat-rating.md` (approved, Mai Do, 2026-01-12). Written 2026-10-07. Stack: Node >=20, `node --test`, no dependencies. Guardrail: `npm test`.
Status: **plan written, nothing built.** The author was away; every question below was answered by recommendation. Review the "Answers" table first.

## Phases

| # | Phase | Covers | Class | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | [Rename `assignee_id` to `owner_id`](phase-01-rename-owner.md) | req 6 | COUNCIL (export contract, DB column) | todo |
| 2 | [Rate a resolved ticket](phase-02-rate-ticket.md) | req 1, 2, 3 | COUNCIL (DB columns); hot path (tenant scoping) | todo |
| 3 | [Email on a low score](phase-03-low-score-email.md) | req 4 | ACT | todo |
| 4 | [Weekly summary job](phase-04-weekly-summary.md) | req 5 | COUNCIL (new stored summary) | todo |
| 5 | [Independent review and close](phase-05-review-close.md) | all | hot-path review | todo |

Order: the rename goes first so no new code is ever written with the old name. Phases 2 to 4 each leave `npm test` green and can ship alone.

## Findings that shaped the plan (anchors from the current code)

- The nightly export writes each ticket row as stored (`src/jobs/export-tickets.js:11-13`). Finance's reporting job reads it and deploys on its own schedule. A plain rename changes their input. Phase 1 keeps the old key in the export for now.
- `notifyAgent` throws when there is no agent (`src/notify.js:6`). A resolved ticket can be unassigned, so a score of 1 or 2 on such a ticket would crash the rating unless guarded (phase 3).
- A closed ticket "is never changed again" (`docs/glossary.md`, Status). Rating a closed ticket would change it, so only resolved tickets can be rated, as the spec says.
- The portal answers "not yours" like "not found" by returning `null` (`src/portal.js:9`). Rating follows that, with the organisation check before any other check.
- Production runs the store interface over Postgres. That adapter is not in this repo, so the column changes (phases 1, 2, 4) are deploy prerequisites listed in each phase, not code here.

## Answers to the validation questions (recommended, not yet confirmed)

| # | Question | Answer used |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Does the rename also change finance's export? | No. The export emits `owner_id` and also `assignee_id` with the same value, marked TEMPORARY, expiry 2026-12-31. Finance is told first; the alias is removed in its own change after they confirm. The spec's "everywhere" is met in our code and storage, not in a file another team reads. |
| 2 | Can a closed ticket be rated? | No. Resolved only. Tickets close after 3 quiet days, so the window is short; a follow-up spec can widen it with a separate ratings record. |
| 3 | Where is the rating stored? | On the ticket row: `rating_score`, `rated_at` (both null until rated). The write is one conditional update, so two simultaneous attempts cannot both win. |
| 4 | Rated ticket has no agent, score 1 or 2? | The rating is stored, no email is queued, no error. |
| 5 | Where does the weekly summary go, and when? | Saved through a new store method `saveCsatSummary`, one row per agent per night: rolling last seven days, keyed by `owner_id` and `week_ending` (the run date). Re-running the same night replaces the row. |
| 6 | Agents with no ratings in the window? | No row. An average over zero ratings has no value. |
| 7 | A rating belongs to which agent? | The ticket's `owner_id` at the time the job runs. Reassigning moves the rating with the ticket. |
| 8 | A rated ticket that is reopened and resolved again? | The first rating stays; a second one is refused (req 2). |

## Execution

Recommended: inline in one session with a checkpoint after each phase, because phases 1, 2 and 4 stop at a COUNCIL point (the migration notes need an owner's yes before deploy, not before code). Subagent-driven is the alternative; it adds nothing for four small phases.

## Self-review against the spec

Coverage: req 1-3 phase 2; req 4 phase 3; req 5 phase 4; req 6 phase 1. Every acceptance line has a named test (see each phase). No placeholders. Names used throughout: `rateTicket` (store and portal), `rating_score`, `rated_at`, `owner_id`, `saveCsatSummary`, `weeklyCsat`.
